WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:06.610
- So, there was this guy
in like seventh grade

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.240
that had a crush on me.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.348
He would kind of like follow
me during school a lot

00:00:12.348 --> 00:00:14.030
just because he liked me

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:17.070
and I saw him at lunch,
you know just like,

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.494
he was hiding behind a trash
can kind of like staring at me

00:00:19.494 --> 00:00:22.490
and I like went up to him
and I said I hate you,

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:23.680
why do you exist?

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.420
Since that day on, he didn't
talk to me or anything

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.320
and this is the probably worst thing

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.690
I've ever said to anyone, so.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.860
I felt really guilty afterwards.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.470
I didn't really say that
I was sorry or anything

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:38.740
because I was just like in middle school

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.200
and it made me look cool

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.110
so I thought it was okay
to say things like that

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.030
but now looking back at it,

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.920
I hope it didn't follow him or anything.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.030
If I saw him today, I would
just like to say sorry

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:50.900
and that he's worth something

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.000
and that he's an important person.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.520
- Most painful thing
I've ever told someone

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.090
was when I was 19 years old

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:59.619
and I told my parents that
I was molested by my brother

00:00:59.619 --> 00:01:02.620
and they didn't believe me.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.215
And it took 20 years later

00:01:05.215 --> 00:01:09.540
for my brother to apologize on his own

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.360
and my mom to this day
still doesn't believe me.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.490
That's the most painful thing.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:16.570
- [Interviewer] You are so comfortable

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:17.800
with talking about it,

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.620
what has got you to this point?

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:20.490
- Therapy.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.110
Therapy and talking about it

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.923
and knowing that,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.950
knowing that the shame is not mine,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.840
knowing that

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.120
I was young.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.763
I didn't, he was older,

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:42.180
when I spoke up I did what
a 19 year old would do,

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.093
go to support

00:01:43.093 --> 00:01:45.010
and when I didn't get
it, I kept looking for it

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.180
because my life mattered and it still does

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.493
and I share it pretty
comfortably these days.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.000
- So a couple years
ago, I lost my daughter

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.810
and everybody had a very strong
connection with my daughter

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:57.960
and it was heartbreaking

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.460
to have to tell each and
every person individually

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.410
what happened to her and why she passed.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:06.766
She passed away of SIDS,
sudden infant death syndrome.

00:02:06.766 --> 00:02:08.320
Yeah, she had a heart condition

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.510
that was passed on from me,

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.793
so yeah it was heartbreaking.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.000
- I told my dying mother

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.940
that I no longer want her in my life,

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.300
that I can no longer support her

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.970
and give her the love and
affection that she wants

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:28.933
'cause she ruined too many chances.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.803
And when she died, I never said goodbye.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.940
I think happiness is
the most selfish thing

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.820
that anyone can do and
sometimes finding your happiness

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.360
means you gotta make those tough decisions

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.790
and let people go.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:47.930
Sometimes happiness isn't always sunshine

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.040
and you know, smiles,

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.410
sometimes it's rain and pain
and suffering and moving on

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.510
and struggling.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:58.940
And I think it really helped
define me and who I am

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.210
and the happiness that I'm
trying to create for myself

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:04.973
and it was tough but it was
the best decision for me.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.660
- So, it's not like a single thing,

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.780
but I used to kinda bully
my brother and call him fat

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.950
and now I wish that I was
more supportive of him

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.233
and didn't matter what
he looked like nothing

00:03:15.233 --> 00:03:17.040
now I feel he looks more,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.160
feels more comfortable
about his body image,

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.750
but I feel like for a long time

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.570
I was part of the problem
with his body image

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:24.767
and I don't feel good about that.

00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:27.117
- [Interviewer] Have you
expressed this to him?

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.760
- You know, now I talk to him about

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.290
how I would only like to support him

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:34.430
but I've never actually told him

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:37.198
that I felt bad for
pretty much bullying him

00:03:37.198 --> 00:03:38.943
when we were younger.

00:03:39.995 --> 00:03:43.872
- In like Freshman year, I just like,

00:03:43.872 --> 00:03:46.150
I broke up with a girlfriend over text.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.210
Like it was the most terrible
way to break up with anybody.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:49.926
I'm not like this person
anymore obviously,

00:03:49.926 --> 00:03:51.320
you go through changes

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.130
and I became a different person you know,

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.410
but I was like, your
ex-boyfriend's really hot,

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:58.430
she was like which one,

00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.720
I was like me.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.750
That was a very painful thing to say.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.530
I think it was a reckless,
not really caring

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.360
and just like, too joking in a way

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:08.830
that you didn't really care

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.850
about other people's feelings, you know.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:11.683
Yeah.

00:04:11.683 --> 00:04:12.516
- [Interviewer] And what changed?

00:04:12.516 --> 00:04:13.349
Do you think that now you

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:15.760
actually do care about people's feelings?

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.736
- Well, I was like
realizing that you know,

00:04:17.736 --> 00:04:19.750
it really matters how
you treat other people.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:22.850
You know, if you're not treating
anything like it's you too.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:25.120
- [Interviewer] So like
treating everyone like you do.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.630
- Everything as it is yourself, yeah.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.780
- Saying I love you to my dad.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.900
I hadn't said I love you to my dad

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.370
in like a really long time

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:38.040
and when I said it during our argument,

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.370
I just like, it didn't feel right

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:44.120
and I just don't think I
ever wanna say it again

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.920
unless I know it's
coming from a good place.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.449
So, I think that's it for me.

00:04:50.449 --> 00:04:52.220
- Before, now you can't really tell,

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.040
but I used to be a little rounder,

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:54.873
a little bit more chubby

00:04:54.873 --> 00:04:56.300
and a lot of people would tell me like

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:57.736
oh, you lost a lot of weight.

00:04:57.736 --> 00:05:00.180
Some people think I've lost
the weight in a healthy way

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.400
but the way I actually lost
it was really unhealthy

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.147
and I was just in a really bad,

00:05:04.147 --> 00:05:05.910
I was in a bad time in my life

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.810
and I just straight up
could not consume food

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:09.810
or eat anything.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.950
So the most painful
thing that I experienced

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:17.160
was having to actually and
truly explain how I lost weight.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.990
It was painful for me to explain

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.733
because it's just kinda like, oh

00:05:20.733 --> 00:05:23.100
yeah that's the reality of it

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.417
and that's actually how I lost everything,

00:05:24.417 --> 00:05:26.010
like all the weight.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.270
But we're good now.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:28.600
We're good now, we're thriving.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:31.280
- I really mad and I told like,

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:32.990
I told my family as a whole

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.450
that they were doing bad
job at being a family.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:36.470
Like I don't know what I was saying,

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.150
I was kinda like mad.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.580
Like I love my family and we fight a lot

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:41.840
but they do everything for me

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:43.340
as much as they can.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:44.270
I talked to them that day,

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:46.100
I was like okay, I'm sorry
I didn't mean to say that.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:47.302
'cause I can't go to bed,

00:05:47.302 --> 00:05:49.370
well I can go to bed being mad at them

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:50.530
but I can't go to bed

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:53.923
having them feel that way about me, upset.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.510
- The most painful
thing I've told somebody

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.423
is I've cheated on you.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:02.960
Something I kinda think
about a lot actually

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:05.100
and how wrong it was

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:06.490
and

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:07.590
how sorry I am.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.736
And it kind of effects where I'm at today.

00:06:09.736 --> 00:06:11.586
- [Interviewer] Did they forgive you?

00:06:13.453 --> 00:06:14.403
- I don't think so.

00:06:17.022 --> 00:06:17.855
- I'm

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.420
that I'm (giggling)

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:29.983
okay wait.

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:32.620
Okay (clears throat).

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:35.680
It was

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.999
coming out as not entirely straight

00:06:41.999 --> 00:06:43.240
to my boyfriend

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:49.480
and the only reason it was just so painful

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:54.480
is 'cause growing up, my
family always kind of teased me

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.020
that I was

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.220
gay

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:04.220
and so I never wanted to admit to myself

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:05.770
and

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:08.860
so I've only came out to my boyfriend

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:09.770
and nobody else

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.320
just 'cause I don't want them

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.933
to have sort of satisfaction
that they were always right.

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:17.653
I

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:23.950
haven't quite admitted that to my family

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:26.350
and some of the people closest to me

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:31.350
but I don't really, I'm not
ashamed of it, so, yeah.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:33.950
- The last time I saw my grandma,

00:07:35.637 --> 00:07:39.510
she was joking with me about seeing my dad

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:40.760
and I blew her off

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.030
and then like a week later, she died.

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:44.110
I really really loved her

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:47.100
and as a kid growing up I
couldn't speak that much Spanish

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.670
and there was like a
language barrier with us

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:54.230
but she would always
call me el chico comico

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:55.160
or something like that

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.160
or she would always just like
be really really nice to me

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:02.160
and I just felt like I couldn't
be like a good grandchild

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:05.560
and I feel like now I singing
a lot in Spanish and stuff

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:07.790
so it's just like I feel
like now that I'm older

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:12.000
I'm trying to at least
like honor her in that way.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.680
- The most painful thing that
I'll probably tell somebody

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:15.587
is to my real dad,

00:08:15.587 --> 00:08:17.393
is that I hated him.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.157
It just really hurts like,

00:08:20.157 --> 00:08:22.709
'cause he tried to treat me better

00:08:22.709 --> 00:08:24.970
and I just didn't let him

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:26.321
and I told him that I hated him

00:08:26.321 --> 00:08:29.140
before when he passed away.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:30.570
- So about eight and a half years ago,

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:33.320
my husband was hit by a
car while riding his bike

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:38.225
and everything basically was
broken from here to his pelvis

00:08:38.225 --> 00:08:41.790
and I had the job of calling
his then 90 year old father

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:43.610
to tell him what happened to his son

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:46.460
and it's really hard to
hear a 90 year old guy

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:49.741
who's a World War II vet
ball on the telephone

00:08:49.741 --> 00:08:53.430
and I had to keep him posted
through the whole recovery

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:57.340
and to this day I cannot
call him on the phone

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:59.610
because whenever I call and he answers

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.270
he thinks something's
really horrible has happened

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:03.930
so someone else has to call
him and hand me the phone

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.570
because he still can't have me call him.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:10.090
You realize that being
a parent never stops

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:12.460
that he was still a 90
year old man with a son

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:14.920
and probably still sees
his son as his little boy

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.700
and he's hundreds of miles away

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:18.570
and his kid's in a hospital broken

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:21.894
and yeah it was a pretty
pretty poignant moment.

00:09:21.894 --> 00:09:23.128
- [Interviewer] Is it you?

00:09:23.128 --> 00:09:24.330
(laughing)

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:29.330
okay, so how does it make
you feel hearing that story?

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.880
- It reminds me of the whole situation.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:38.220
Everything she says about you
never stop being a parent.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:39.800
One that jumps out at me

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:42.430
and I can't believe it still
bothers me to this day.

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:45.640
12 years ago or more, my
daughter who's in her 30s now,

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:49.050
was in college and she
was giving short answers

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.090
to family members when they'd call

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.250
and just really not being too interested

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:55.400
and instead of actually asking her

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:56.930
what was going on or whatever,

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:59.000
I read her Riot Act over the phone

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:01.910
and brought her to tears

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:05.140
and I felt so terrible for doing that

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:09.720
and she couldn't, she could
barely talk at the other end

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:10.553
because I did that.

00:10:10.553 --> 00:10:12.800
So, there's a series of
phone calls afterwards

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:14.127
where we made amends and all that stuff

00:10:14.127 --> 00:10:16.880
but she was so far from
home it seemed like

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:18.520
and I couldn't believe that I made her cry

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:20.481
over something so trivial.

00:10:20.481 --> 00:10:21.502
Yeah, that's it.

00:10:21.502 --> 00:10:22.335
- Okay,

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:29.100
when we were going to
help my sister's boyfriend

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:31.064
propose to my sister,

00:10:31.064 --> 00:10:35.770
my brother and I got into
a fight on the way up there

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:41.400
and I told him that her
fiance didn't even like him

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.844
and that he wasn't really
a part of the family

00:10:43.844 --> 00:10:45.260
and stuff like that.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:46.900
It was really awful.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:48.360
Like the moment it came out of my mouth,

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:49.870
I was like oh my god,

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:51.958
can't believe I would ever
say something like that.

00:10:51.958 --> 00:10:54.998
Especially in such a
beautiful moment you know.

00:10:54.998 --> 00:10:56.430
They're best friends

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:58.620
and my brother looks up to him so much

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:02.270
so it was a really low jab.

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:04.210
He got really silent and that's how I knew

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:07.530
that I had like really crossed the line

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:10.500
because usually he would
like yell at me or react.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:11.740
I pretty much apologized

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:14.450
like the moment it came out of my mouth

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:18.230
'cause I realized that was just awful.

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.910
So, yeah, we're good now though.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:23.910
It makes me more conscious
of everything that I say

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:26.800
because I've seen how
hurt other people can be

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:28.263
by just your words.

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:37.700
- So, I think reversing the question

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:39.614
which is what we did on today's episode,

00:11:39.614 --> 00:11:43.520
really shines light on the
fact that we're all human

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:46.100
and sometimes we make bad decisions

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:48.220
and use the wrong words.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:49.640
And obviously that's not okay,

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:50.780
that's not what I'm saying,

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.010
but you can still be a good person

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:54.674
who makes bad decisions

00:11:54.674 --> 00:11:57.480
and the important thing is to realize

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.400
that you did something bad

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:00.840
or did something hurtful

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:02.520
and try not to do it again

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:03.950
and obviously ask for forgiveness.

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:05.852
So, that's pretty much it.

00:12:05.852 --> 00:12:07.900
Thank you guys for watching

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:09.363
and I'll see you next week.

